## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design scalable, resilient multi-region systems with per-tenant isolation, hot-tenant mitigation, and cost control—core competencies for distributed systems engineers at senior levels.

## Key Concepts

- Time-based shard design and tenant isolation strategies
- Multi-region replication with quorum-based consistency
- Hot-tenant mitigation and dynamic shard rebalancing
- Data lifecycle management: tiered storage, retention, compaction, and archival

## Code Example

```javascript
function shardKey(tenantId, timestamp) {
  const hourBucket = Math.floor(timestamp / 3600000);
  return `${tenantId}:${hourBucket}`;
}

class TimeSeriesStore {
  constructor(regions, replicationFactor = 3) {
    this.regions = regions;
    this.replicationFactor = replicationFactor;
    this.quorum = Math.floor(replicationFactor / 2) + 1;
  }

  async write(tenantId, timestamp, data) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const writes = replicas.map(region => this.writeToRegion(region, key, data));
    await Promise.all(writes);
    return this.verifyQuorum(writes);
  }

  selectReplicas(shardKey) {
    return this.regions.slice(0, this.replicationFactor);
  }

  async read(tenantId, timestamp) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const reads = replicas.map(region => this.readFromRegion(region, key));
    return this.mergeResults(await Promise.all(reads));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of cross-service coordination using Saga, CQRS, and event sourcing in realistic e-commerce workflows. It emphasizes long-running process handling, compensation, and replay-safe state.

## Key Concepts

- Saga state machine with compensations for long-running business processes
- Event sourcing and CQRS read/write separation
- Idempotency, correlation IDs, and versioning to handle retries and out-of-order events
- Compensation patterns (ReleaseInventory, RefundPayment) and failure healing
- Snapshotting to bound event stores and speed reads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator tick (high-level)
function onEvent(state, event) {
  if (state.orderId !== event.orderId) return state
  switch (event.type) {
    case 'CartVerified':
      return { ...state, currentStep: 'InventoryReserved' }
    case 'InventoryReserved':
      return { ...state, currentStep: 'PaymentHeld' }
    case 'PaymentFailed':
      // trigger compensations
      return { ...state, compensations: ['ReleaseInventory','OrderRollback'] }
    case 'OrderCommitted':
      return { ...state, currentStep: 'Completed' }
    default:
      return state
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ensure idempotency across services with duplicate or replayed events?
- What strategies handle partial failures when a compensation itself fails?
- How would you design read-model projections to support fast queries while keeping the event log strictly append-only?

